          '**Imaging Urgency**:\n- MESCC is an **oncologic emergency**. MRI of the **entire spine** (with and without gadolinium) must be obtained within **24 hours** of clinical suspicion — ideally within hours. Whole-spine imaging is essential because **10-38% of patients have multilevel disease**.\n- If MRI is contraindicated, CT myelography is an alternative.\n- Do NOT delay imaging for other workup. Do NOT wait for plain radiographs (sensitivity only 60-70%).\n\n**Expected MRI Findings**: Epidural soft tissue mass at T7-T8 with spinal cord compression and possible signal change within the cord (edema or myelomalacia). Vertebral body involvement with pathologic fracture is common.\n\n**Bilsky Epidural Spinal Cord Compression Scale**:\n- Grade 0: Bone-only disease\n- Grade 1a-c: Epidural impingement without cord compression\n- Grade 2: Cord compression with visible CSF\n- Grade 3: Cord compression without visible CSF\nThis patient likely has **Grade 2-3** compression given his clinical findings.\n\n**Tokuhashi Revised Prognostic Scoring System** (total 0-15 points):\n- General condition (KPS): 1 (KPS 40-70%)\n- Number of extraspinal bone metastases: 1 (1-2 sites)\n- Number of vertebral body metastases: 1 (1-2)\n- Major internal organ metastases: 1 (no major organ mets currently)\n- Primary cancer site: 1 (lung — poor prognosis primary)\n- Spinal cord palsy: 1 (incomplete — Frankel C-D)\n- **Estimated total: 6/15**\n\n**Tokuhashi Interpretation**:\n- 0-8 points: Predicted survival <6 months → palliative/non-operative treatment (radiation)\n- 9-11 points: Predicted survival 6-12 months → consider palliative surgery or radiation\n- 12-15 points: Predicted survival >12 months → consider excisional surgery\n\nWith a score of approximately 6, **palliative radiation therapy** would be the primary recommendation, though surgical decompression should be considered given his ambulatory status and incomplete deficit.',
